INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('1f184272d0fba563eae8','Amedex 2023.pdf','971','Mixed/Unclassified','An 18-year-old girl presents to your office with painful menses for the past 12 months. She has been prescribed NSAIDs initially and then OCPs after the NSAIDs failed to control her
symptoms. OCP has been unable to control the painful menses as well. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate next step in management?','{"A":"Transabdominal ultrasound.","B":"Transvaginal ultrasound.","C":"CT scan of the pelvis and abdomen.","D":"Dilation and curettage.","E":"Laparoscopy."}','B','The main complaint in this patient is with painful periods, also termed dysmenorrhea.
Dysmenorrhea can be divided into two broad categories of primary and secondary. Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as recurrent, crampy pain occurring with menses in the
absence of identifiable pelvic pathology. Secondary dysmenorrhea is menstrual pain associated with underlying pelvic pathology such as endometriosis.
Primary dysmenorrhea usually begins in adolescence after the establishment of ovulatory cycles. Primary dysmenorrhea is caused by myometrial activity and consequent uterine
ischemia. This myometrial activity is modulated and augmented by prostaglandin synthesis; therefore, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), by inhibition of prostaglandin
synthesis, are the first-line treatment option.
Oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) are used as second-line if NSAIDs fail. OCPs can also be used as the treatment of choice (first-line) if contraception is desired as well. OCPs prevent
menstrual pain by suppressing ovulation and decreasing uterine prostaglandin levels. An additional mechanism is the reduction of menstrual flow after several months of use.
If treatment with either of these medications fails after 2 or 3 menstrual cycles, a course of treatment with the other modality should be used. Treatment with both hormonal
contraceptives and NSAIDs may be effective in women who do not respond to either drug alone.
Adolescents who fail to respond to first- or second-line treatment, have recurrent symptoms, or have worsening symptoms should be re-evaluated for the possible causes of
secondary dysmenorrhea such as endometriosis, uterine leiomyomas, polyps, or other pelvic pathologies.
In this patient, treatment has failed despite use of both NSAIDs and OCP; therefore, secondary dysmenorrhea should be considered and investigated. When pelvic pathology is
suspected, abdominal and transvaginal ultrasonography should be used as first-line investigation. They are inexpensive, effective, and readily available. Transvaginal ultrasound is
more accurate and the preferred option if possible.
(Option A) Transabdominal ultrasound is inferior to transvaginal ultrasound for evaluation of dysmenorrhea; but it can be used instead of transvaginal ultrasound if transvaginal is
not possible e.g., in virgin girls.
(Option C) CT scan and MRI are not routinely used for assessment of secondary dysmenorrhea.
(Options D and E) Dilation and curettage or laparoscopy might be indicated at some stage after initial evaluation points towards a specific pathology such as endometriosis or
endometrial problems, but not as an initial step.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"930","duplicate_group_id":"exact-75d7d1df8ca2","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"1f184272d0fba563eae8","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":971,"source_qid":"930"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"native","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"1f184272d0fba563eae8","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_year":2023,"source_page":971,"source_qid":"930","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"An 18-year-old girl presents to your office with painful menses for the past 12 months. She has been prescribed NSAIDs initially and then OCPs after the NSAIDs failed to control her\nsymptoms. OCP has been unable to control the painful menses as well. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate next step in management?","options":{"A":"Transabdominal ultrasound.","B":"Transvaginal ultrasound.","C":"CT scan of the pelvis and abdomen.","D":"Dilation and curettage.","E":"Laparoscopy."},"source_answer":"B","source_explanation":"The main complaint in this patient is with painful periods, also termed dysmenorrhea.\nDysmenorrhea can be divided into two broad categories of primary and secondary. Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as recurrent, crampy pain occurring with menses in the\nabsence of identifiable pelvic pathology. Secondary dysmenorrhea is menstrual pain associated with underlying pelvic pathology such as endometriosis.\nPrimary dysmenorrhea usually begins in adolescence after the establishment of ovulatory cycles. Primary dysmenorrhea is caused by myometrial activity 